      "narrative": "Maricopa County’s residential market is roughly flat to modestly higher on price, with multiple June-July 2026 reports showing year-over-year gains in the mid-single digits or low single digits, though some local commentary describes pricing as mostly stable rather than surging.[2][3][7] Inventory remains constrained but not absent: reports show active listings in the low 20,000s or a few months of supply, while days on market have edged up, which points to a slower, more balanced pace than a hot seller’s market.[2][3][1] The buyer-seller dynamic is mixed: sales and pending activity are still solid, but the rise in days on market and the lower pending volume year over year suggest buyers have a bit more leverage than during peak boom periods, even as sellers still benefit from relatively steady demand.[2][7] Local drivers remain supportive, including continued population inflow into Greater Phoenix, ongoing construction and new listings, and a broad employment base tied to the metro’s large employers and industrial growth, while some reports also note investor activity and foreclosure filings moving in opposite directions depending on the segment.[7][13][18]",
