      "narrative": "Pinal County’s residential market is showing flat-to-slightly softer pricing rather than a surge: Realtor.com’s county page shows a median listing price of $394.9K in May 2026, down year over year, while ARMLS-linked regional reporting says Greater Phoenix median prices were still edging up in June even as inventory remained constrained.[2][1] Inventory is still a central theme, with Realtor.com showing 7.5K active listings for the county and regional reporting noting that new listings and overall supply have not fully caught up with demand.[2][1] The buyer-seller balance is more active than overheated: homes are taking longer to sell, and the market is still giving buyers more choice than during the pandemic peak, but contract activity remains solid enough to keep sellers engaged.[2][1] Local demand is being supported by population growth in Pinal County, which ranked among the nation’s fastest-growing counties over five years, and by continued in-migration into fast-growing communities such as Casa Grande, Maricopa, and Florence.[1][4][8] Regional reports also point to ongoing new construction across the Phoenix-Pinal corridor and broader employment/migration pull from metro-area growth, which together keep housing demand anchored even as pricing cools from earlier highs.[9][8]",
